// KQL keywords are upper case — the lexer defines them as literals and ID is lower-case only,
// so a lower-case `distinct` never reaches the DISTINCT alternative of the `operator` rule. It
// becomes a *custom* operator instead, and that used to be worse than the escape hatch it looked
// like: nothing was passed through, because negatedOperatorTemplate normalises its argument and
// returned the ANSI `IS NOT DISTINCT FROM`, while the MariaDB (`<=>`) and Oracle (`DECODE`)
// overrides compare exactly and never fired. Both engines reject that form — ERROR 1064 and
// ORA-00908 — and the "not a KQL operator" warning was suppressed by the same upper-case retry.
// A case-only difference from a real operator is now a named error naming the correct spelling.
FIND customers c, c orders o
FILTER NOT c.region distinct o.ship_region
FETCH c.customer_id
